'''Bell’s disease, finch.''' See ''disease'', ''finch''.

'''bell-shaped''' (bel′shāpt), ''a.'' Having the form of a bell, or of a somewhat deep vessel whose lip turns out and then begins to turn in again; specifically, in ''bot.'', campanulate. See cut
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bell-shaped Parabola.}}}}
under ''Campanula.''—{{smaller|'''Bell-shaped parabola''', a divergent parabola having neither crunode nor cusp. Some geometricians, without sufficient reason or authority, restrict the name to those divergent parabolas to which from some points of the plane six real tangents can be drawn.—'''Pure bell-shaped parabola''', one which constitutes the entire real part of a cubic curve of the sixth class.}}

'''bell-sound''' (bel′sound), ''n.'' In auscultation, a peculiar sound indicative of pneumothorax. {{smaller|It may be observed by applying a small piece of metal, as a coin, to the affected part of the chest, and striking it with a second piece, when a clear, bell-like sound is heard through the stethoscope applied in the vicinity.}}

'''bell-telegraph''' (bel′tel″ē-grȧf), ''n.'' '''1.''' A telegraphic apparatus in which two differently toned bells take the place of a vibrating needle in giving the signals.—'''2.''' An annunciator; a fire- or burglar-alarm.

'''bell-tower''' (bel′tou″ėr), ''n.'' A tower of any kind built to contain one or more bells. See cut

readings.c1s3:
under ''campanile.''
{{smaller block|The unsurpassed ''bell-tower'' of the Duomo, known and admired by all men as the Campanile of Giotto, [is] the most splendid memorial of the arts of Florence.
{{right|''C. E. Norton'', Church-building in Middle Ages, p. 222.}}}}

'''bell-trap''' (bel′trap), ''n.'' A small stench-trap, usually fixed over the waste-pipe of a sink or other inlet to a drain. {{smaller|The foul air is prevented from rising by an inverted cup or bell, the lips of which dip into a chamber filled with water surrounding the top of the pipe.}}

'''bell-turret''' (bel′tur″et), ''n.'' A turret containing a bell-chamber, and usually crowned with
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bell-turret.—Abbaye-aux-Hommes, Caen, Normandy.}}}}
a spire or other ornamental feature. {{smaller|In medieval architecture the lower part of such turrets is often used as a staircase. A bell-turret is distinguished from a bell-cote in that the former always appears upon the ground-plan of the building to which it belongs.}}

